WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.530
Lesson 4 takes the single-wire motor effect and builds a machine out of it.

2
00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:13.060
A current loop in a magnetic field experiences forces on opposite sides of the coil.

3
00:00:13.060 --> 00:00:19.590
Those forces act in opposite directions and form a turning pair, so the coil rotates instead of sliding away.

4
00:00:19.590 --> 00:00:26.120
Once students really see that pair, later details become easier.

5
00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:32.650
More turns mean more repeating force pairs, larger current means stronger forces, and a stronger field means stronger turning effect.

6
00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:39.180
The commutator then keeps the overall rotation direction going instead of letting the coil stall.

7
00:00:39.180 --> 00:00:45.710
This lesson stays rich by keeping those ideas tied together instead of teaching them as disconnected motor facts.

8
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:52.240
Keep this takeaway in view: A motor is easiest to understand when it is read as torque built from two magnetic side-kicks.

9
00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:58.770
Opposite sides means opposite forces.

10
00:00:58.770 --> 00:01:05.300
The two active sides of the coil feel forces in opposite directions.

11
00:01:05.300 --> 00:01:11.830
Combined result means turning effect.

12
00:01:11.830 --> 00:01:18.360
The pair of forces makes torque about the axle instead of one straight translation.

13
00:01:18.360 --> 00:01:24.809
This diagram matters because it turns the motor from a named device into a readable mechanism.

14
00:01:24.809 --> 00:01:31.258
The left side of the loop is pushed one way and the right side is pushed the opposite way.

15
00:01:31.258 --> 00:01:37.707
Because those forces act at different sides of the axle, they produce a turning effect.

16
00:01:37.707 --> 00:01:44.156
That is the heart of the motor.

17
00:01:44.156 --> 00:01:50.604
The commutator then changes the current connection each half-turn so the torque keeps helping the same overall rotation.

18
00:01:50.604 --> 00:01:57.053
Once the learner has this picture, the worked example's turning-moment formula feels like a compact version of the diagram rather than a sudden new topic.

19
00:01:57.053 --> 00:02:03.502
Keep this takeaway in view: The spin-frame picture makes the motor a force-pair story instead of a mystery device.

20
00:02:03.502 --> 00:02:09.951
Left side: One side of the loop feels one magnetic side-kick.

21
00:02:09.951 --> 00:02:16.400
Right side: The opposite side feels the opposite magnetic side-kick.

22
00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:22.495
A door analogy helps because it emphasizes how separated forces cause turning.

23
00:02:22.495 --> 00:02:28.591
If one side of a door is pushed one way while another force acts on the opposite side, the result is not just translation of the whole door.

24
00:02:28.591 --> 00:02:34.686
The hinge makes the result a turn.

25
00:02:34.686 --> 00:02:40.782
A motor coil is similar in spirit.

26
00:02:40.782 --> 00:02:46.877
The magnetic field produces opposite forces on opposite sides of the loop, and the axle plays the role of the hinge.

27
00:02:46.877 --> 00:02:52.973
This analogy gives the learner a physical feel for torque before the mathematics is added.

28
00:02:52.973 --> 00:02:59.068
Keep this takeaway in view: The door analogy helps because it turns the abstract idea of torque into an everyday force-pair story.

29
00:02:59.068 --> 00:03:05.164
Separated pushes means force pair.

30
00:03:05.164 --> 00:03:11.259
Two forces applied at different sides can create a turn even if the net straight push is not the point.

31
00:03:11.259 --> 00:03:17.355
Hinge means like the axle.

32
00:03:17.355 --> 00:03:23.450
The axle is the turning reference about which the coil rotates.

33
00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:28.345
At maximum turning effect, the torque on a rectangular coil can be written as tau equals B I N A.

34
00:03:28.345 --> 00:03:33.241
The symbols capture what the diagram has already shown.

35
00:03:33.241 --> 00:03:38.136
A stronger field means stronger magnetic forces.

36
00:03:38.136 --> 00:03:43.031
A larger current means stronger forces on the coil sides.

37
00:03:43.031 --> 00:03:47.927
More turns mean more copies of the same force-pair effect.

38
00:03:47.927 --> 00:03:52.822
A larger area gives the forces more leverage for turning.

39
00:03:52.822 --> 00:03:57.717
The formula therefore belongs to the machine picture.

40
00:03:57.717 --> 00:04:02.613
It does not replace it.

41
00:04:02.613 --> 00:04:07.508
Students who remember that usually handle motor questions much more confidently.

42
00:04:07.508 --> 00:04:12.403
Keep this takeaway in view: The coil-torque formula is easiest to trust when it is read as a summary of the force-pair mechanism.

43
00:04:12.403 --> 00:04:17.299
Why B matters: A stronger field gives stronger magnetic forces on the active sides.

44
00:04:17.299 --> 00:04:22.194
Why N matters: More turns mean more loop segments contributing to the same turning effect.

45
00:04:22.194 --> 00:04:27.089
tau = B I N A.

46
00:04:27.089 --> 00:04:31.985
larger B, I, N, or A => larger turning effect.

47
00:04:31.985 --> 00:04:36.880
Use the torque formula after you have identified why the coil turns at all.

48
00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.082
A dependable routine for Lesson 4 has four moves.

49
00:04:42.082 --> 00:04:47.283
First identify the two active sides of the coil.

50
00:04:47.283 --> 00:04:52.485
Second state that the sides feel opposite magnetic forces.

51
00:04:52.485 --> 00:04:57.687
Third describe the result as torque about the axle.

52
00:04:57.687 --> 00:05:02.888
Fourth use the formula and, if needed, explain the commutator as the reason the turning keeps the same overall sense.

53
00:05:02.888 --> 00:05:08.090
This order matters because the formula alone does not explain why the motor rotates.

54
00:05:08.090 --> 00:05:13.292
The mechanism has to stay visible.

55
00:05:13.292 --> 00:05:18.493
Keep this takeaway in view: Pair, then torque, then control is the safest reasoning pattern for motor questions.

56
00:05:18.493 --> 00:05:23.695
Step 1 means find the active sides.

57
00:05:23.695 --> 00:05:28.897
The opposite sides in the field are the important current-carrying sections.

58
00:05:28.897 --> 00:05:34.098
Step 2 means state the force pair.

59
00:05:34.098 --> 00:05:39.300
Each side feels a magnetic force in the opposite direction.

60
00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:44.748
A motor is not best explained by saying one magnetic force pushes the coil around.

61
00:05:44.748 --> 00:05:50.196
That picture hides the real cause of rotation.

62
00:05:50.196 --> 00:05:55.644
The real cause is that opposite sides of the coil feel opposite forces, and those forces act at different positions relative to the axle.

63
00:05:55.644 --> 00:06:01.092
That is what creates the turning effect.

64
00:06:01.092 --> 00:06:06.540
Once the learner sees that, torque becomes a natural word instead of a forced vocabulary item.

65
00:06:06.540 --> 00:06:11.988
Keep this takeaway in view: Motors turn because of a force pair, not because of one force pushing the coil around.

66
00:06:11.988 --> 00:06:17.436
Wrong picture means one push.

67
00:06:17.436 --> 00:06:22.884
A single-force picture misses why the motion is rotational.

68
00:06:22.884 --> 00:06:28.332
Right picture means force pair.

69
00:06:28.332 --> 00:06:33.780
Opposite forces on opposite sides create a turning moment.

70
00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:39.179
Without the commutator, the current direction in the coil would not swap at the right moment, and the torque would no longer keep helping the same overall rotation.

71
00:06:39.179 --> 00:06:44.578
The coil could stall or rock instead of spinning continuously.

72
00:06:44.578 --> 00:06:49.977
That is why the commutator is not just a hardware detail.

73
00:06:49.977 --> 00:06:55.376
It is part of the reasoning chain that explains continuous motor action.

74
00:06:55.376 --> 00:07:00.775
Giving it its own scene keeps the lesson from ending too early.

75
00:07:00.775 --> 00:07:06.174
Keep this takeaway in view: The commutator matters because it keeps the torque helping rather than cancelling.

76
00:07:06.174 --> 00:07:11.573
What it does means swaps connections.

77
00:07:11.573 --> 00:07:16.972
The commutator reverses the current connection each half-turn.

78
00:07:16.972 --> 00:07:22.371
Why that helps means torque keeps assisting.

79
00:07:22.371 --> 00:07:27.770
The turning effect stays in the same overall rotational sense.

80
00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:31.236
Ask four checks.

81
00:07:31.236 --> 00:07:34.702
Do opposite sides of the coil feel opposite forces?

82
00:07:34.702 --> 00:07:38.168
Yes.

83
00:07:38.168 --> 00:07:41.634
Do those forces create torque rather than one simple sideways motion?

84
00:07:41.634 --> 00:07:45.100
Yes.

85
00:07:45.100 --> 00:07:48.566
Do larger current and more turns increase the turning effect?

86
00:07:48.566 --> 00:07:52.032
Yes.

87
00:07:52.032 --> 00:07:55.498
And does the commutator help keep the same overall rotation direction?

88
00:07:55.498 --> 00:07:58.964
Yes.

89
00:07:58.964 --> 00:08:02.430
Those checks confirm that the learner is ready for the motor calculation that follows.

90
00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:05.896
Keep this takeaway in view: The quick check confirms that motor torque is now being read as a mechanism, not a slogan.

91
00:08:05.896 --> 00:08:09.362
Coil check means opposite forces.

92
00:08:09.362 --> 00:08:12.828
The two active sides of the loop feel forces in opposite directions.

93
00:08:12.828 --> 00:08:16.294
Torque check means turning effect.

94
00:08:16.294 --> 00:08:19.760
The result is rotation about the axle, not simple translation.

95
00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:25.896
The worked example that follows is easier if you start with the mechanism instead of the equation.

96
00:08:25.896 --> 00:08:32.032
Opposite sides of the coil feel opposite magnetic forces, those forces create torque, and the size of that torque grows with field strength, current, turn count, and coil area.

97
00:08:32.032 --> 00:08:38.168
Once those ideas are active, the turning-moment formula looks like a summary rather than a surprise.

98
00:08:38.168 --> 00:08:44.304
That is exactly the mental state the example needs.

99
00:08:44.304 --> 00:08:50.440
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example becomes shorter once the motor is already understood as a force-pair machine.

100
00:08:50.440 --> 00:08:56.576
First move: Say why the coil turns rather than slides.

101
00:08:56.576 --> 00:09:02.712
Second move: Identify which factors make the turning effect larger.

102
00:09:02.712 --> 00:09:08.848
identify the opposite-force pair.

103
00:09:08.848 --> 00:09:14.984
translate the pair into torque about the axle.

104
00:09:14.984 --> 00:09:21.120
Do not start with the formula before the turning-pair mechanism is visible.
